 \begin{abstract}
-Stochastic simulation of epidemic spread in large contact networks often lacks information about specific contacts between individuals. Aggregate data on population movements and connections between cities or other communities may nevertheless be available. Internal community contact networks can be approximately reconstructed from known structural characteristics, while the specific contacts between communities remain unknown. We study a two-scale stochastic simulation method (\TS{}) that combines detailed local Gillespie simulators with aggregate inter-community transmission rates, without explicitly representing inter-community contacts.
-
-We prove that the ideal two-scale process is equivalent to exact simulation on a surrogate network with uniform inter-community connections. We identify the mechanisms of discrepancy from the full-network model and establish that the first infection export is stochastically no later than in the full-network model under uniformly random bridge-node placement in the theoretical setting considered.
-
-We assess the method's accuracy by comparing it with full-network Gillespie simulation on synthetic SI networks, including 48 configurations of chains of ten communities. The experiments showed close time distributions when infection spread rapidly within communities: in a chain of ten cliques, the normalised Wasserstein distance after nine inter-community crossings was $3.34\%$. However, discrepancies increased substantially in communities with longer internal paths and lower connectivity, reaching $40.9\%$ in the most discrepant configuration. Their magnitude also depended on community size and the number of inter-community edges. The proposed measure of this ratio showed a strong rank association with the magnitude of the discrepancies (Spearman correlation $0.91$).
-
-The results support the possibility of reproducing temporal characteristics close to those of full-network simulation without knowing the specific structure of inter-community contacts, and help identify conditions under which this approximation is appropriate.
+Stochastic epidemic simulation often lacks information about individual contacts. Aggregate data on movements between communities may nevertheless be available. Internal contact networks can be approximately reconstructed from structural characteristics, while specific inter-community contacts remain unknown. We study a two-scale stochastic simulation method (\TS{}) that combines detailed local Gillespie simulators with aggregate inter-community transmission rates, without explicitly representing inter-community contacts. We prove that the ideal two-scale process is equivalent to exact simulation on a surrogate network with uniform inter-community connections. We identify the mechanisms of discrepancy from the full-network model and establish that the first infection export is stochastically no later than in the full-network model under uniformly random bridge-node placement in the theoretical setting considered. We assess the method's accuracy by comparing it with full-network Gillespie simulation on synthetic SI networks, including 48 configurations of chains of ten communities. The experiments showed close time distributions when infection spread rapidly within communities: in a chain of ten cliques, the normalised Wasserstein distance after nine inter-community crossings was $3.34\%$. However, discrepancies increased substantially in communities with longer internal paths and lower connectivity, reaching $40.9\%$ in the most discrepant configuration. Their magnitude also depended on community size and the number of inter-community edges. The proposed time-scale measure showed a strong rank association with the magnitude of the discrepancies (Spearman correlation $0.91$). These results help identify conditions under which aggregate connections reproduce temporal characteristics close to full-network simulation without specifying individual inter-community contacts.
 \end{abstract}
